1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,655
Both questions are worth asking, and the order matters more than people expect.

2
00:00:04,655 --> 00:00:08,850
It is tempting to end the last lecture's story at "the scope should have been

3
00:00:08,850 --> 00:00:10,267
narrower," and stop there.

4
00:00:10,267 --> 00:00:14,517
That is true, and it is also not the most important thing the incident proves.

5
00:00:14,517 --> 00:00:18,876
The more important question is what actually held when the first line of defence

6
00:00:18,876 --> 00:00:19,257
failed.

7
00:00:19,257 --> 00:00:23,780
The two questions produce two different futures, which is why the distinction earns

8
00:00:23,780 --> 00:00:24,325
a lecture.

9
00:00:24,325 --> 00:00:28,684
Reviews that only ask what should have stopped it earlier end in resolutions: be

10
00:00:28,684 --> 00:00:32,008
more careful, read consent screens, never skip the worksheet.

11
00:00:32,008 --> 00:00:36,530
All true, all fragile, because they repair the person, and the person was never the

12
00:00:36,530 --> 00:00:37,293
reliable part.

13
00:00:37,293 --> 00:00:41,598
Asking what held ends somewhere sturdier: it finds the design that did not care

14
00:00:41,598 --> 00:00:46,011
whether anyone was careful that Thursday, and tells you to build more of that, in

15
00:00:46,011 --> 00:00:48,463
places your future carelessness cannot reach.

16
00:00:48,463 --> 00:00:50,152
Resolutions decay in a quarter.

17
00:00:50,152 --> 00:00:51,024
Wiring does not.

18
00:00:51,024 --> 00:00:55,438
You have heard that principle twice now; this is what it looks like the day it is

19
00:00:55,438 --> 00:00:56,146
load bearing.

20
00:00:56,696 --> 00:01:01,173
This is the whole argument of Section 6, proven under real conditions instead of

21
00:01:01,173 --> 00:01:02,684
just asserted in a lecture.

22
00:01:02,684 --> 00:01:04,195
A wrong scope is a mistake.

23
00:01:04,195 --> 00:01:08,895
A wrong scope on an agent that can only draft is a mistake that stays small, because

24
00:01:08,895 --> 00:01:13,428
the thing that actually causes harm, sending something, was never possible in the

25
00:01:13,428 --> 00:01:14,099
first place.

26
00:01:14,099 --> 00:01:17,849
Why did one guardrail's failure tell you nothing about the other's?

27
00:01:17,849 --> 00:01:22,381
Because the two were independent, and the independence was partly about when each

28
00:01:22,381 --> 00:01:23,388
decision got made.

29
00:01:23,388 --> 00:01:27,865
The scope was decided in a hurry, mid-flow, on the busy Thursday, by exactly the

30
00:01:27,865 --> 00:01:30,943
version of Priya most likely to wave something through.

31
00:01:30,943 --> 00:01:35,364
The action level was decided five weeks earlier, on day one, calm, with the job

32
00:01:35,364 --> 00:01:38,050
description open, before any temptation existed.

33
00:01:38,050 --> 00:01:42,582
Layers protect you when they cannot all fail for the same reason, and "decided by

34
00:01:42,582 --> 00:01:47,171
different versions of you" is one of the cheapest independences available: put the

35
00:01:47,171 --> 00:01:51,704
irreversibility guard where your calmest self placed it, and the hurried self who

36
00:01:51,704 --> 00:01:56,236
mis-scopes a credential cannot also have removed it, because the hurried self was

37
00:01:56,236 --> 00:01:56,908
never asked.

38
00:01:57,458 --> 00:02:01,650
That sentence, "you cannot always get the first one right," is not pessimism.

39
00:02:01,650 --> 00:02:06,059
It is the entire reason a single guardrail is never enough, however carefully you

40
00:02:06,059 --> 00:02:06,984
think you set it.

41
00:02:06,984 --> 00:02:11,448
Be honest about the price of layers, because there is one: each adds friction, and

42
00:02:11,448 --> 00:02:14,660
friction is why people strip guardrails in the first place.

43
00:02:14,660 --> 00:02:17,817
The craft is choosing layers whose cost you stop noticing.

44
00:02:17,817 --> 00:02:19,777
Draft-only costs a click per output.

45
00:02:19,777 --> 00:02:23,152
The review read costs minutes you were partly spending anyway.

46
00:02:23,152 --> 00:02:25,492
A scoped credential costs ten minutes once.

47
00:02:25,492 --> 00:02:30,011
Stack those three and you have real depth for almost nothing, which is why they are

48
00:02:30,011 --> 00:02:31,480
this course's standard kit.

49
00:02:31,480 --> 00:02:35,999
A gate on every trivial action, by contrast, is a layer people quietly route around

50
00:02:35,999 --> 00:02:40,517
by Friday, and a routed-around guardrail is worse than none, because it still shows

51
00:02:40,517 --> 00:02:41,932
up on paper as protection.

52
00:02:42,482 --> 00:02:46,997
Four agents, one real incident, and a scope error that never became a disaster.

53
00:02:46,997 --> 00:02:51,570
That is the standard the rest of this course holds itself to, and notice it is a

54
00:02:51,570 --> 00:02:56,371
standard about outcomes, not perfection: the method did not prevent the mistake, the

55
00:02:56,371 --> 00:03:00,315
method made the mistake survivable, and those are different promises.

56
00:03:00,315 --> 00:03:04,202
Only the second one is honest, and it is the one worth building for.

57
00:03:04,202 --> 00:03:08,774
Next lecture closes the section, and Section 9 turns to the number every manager

58
00:03:08,774 --> 00:03:13,004
eventually asks for: what this actually costs, and what it actually saves.
